## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design environment-scoped Terraform with proper naming hygiene, state isolation, encryption, and CI validation gates - critical skills for production infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- Wrapper module pattern for environment injection (dev/stage/prod)
- S3 bucket with server-side encryption (AES256) enabled by default
- CloudWatch log group for application logs
- Backend state per environment: state/${environment}/logs.tfstate
- CI gate enforcing naming convention (environment in bucket name) and encryption enforcement

## Complete Code Example

**variables.tf (wrapper)**
```hcl
variable "environment" {
  type        = string
  description = "Environment: dev, stage, or prod"
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "stage", "prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "Environment must be dev, stage, or prod."
  }
}
variable "project" {
  type        = string
  description = "Project name prefix"
}
variable "region" {
  type        = string
  default     = "us-east-1"
}
```

**main.tf (child module - s3_logs)**
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "logs" {
  bucket = "${var.project}-${var.environment}-logs"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "logs" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id

  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm = "AES256"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_log_group" "app_logs" {
  name              = "/${var.project}/${var.environment}/logs"
  retention_in_days = var.environment == "prod" ? 90 : 7

  tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
  }
}

variable "project" { type = string }
variable "environment" { type = string }
```

**main.tf (wrapper)**
```hcl
module "logs_infrastructure" {
  source = "./modules/s3-logs"
  project     = var.project
  environment = var.environment
}
```

**backend.tf (partial configuration)**
```hcl
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    key            = "logs.tfstate"
    encrypt        = true
    bucket         = "my-terraform-state-bucket"
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-locks"
  }
}
```

**CI Gate - validate-terraform.yml**
```yaml
name: Terraform Validation
on: [pull_request]
jobs:
  validate:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3
        with:
          terraform_version: 1.6

      - name: Init terraform
        run: terraform init -backend-config="key=state/${{ matrix.environment }}/logs.tfstate"
        env:
          AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}
          AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}
        matrix:
          environment: [dev, stage, prod]

      - name: Validate bucket naming
        run: |
          BUCKET_NAME=$(terraform output -raw bucket_name)
          if [[ "$BUCKET_NAME" != *"${{ matrix.environment }}"* ]]; then
            echo "Error: Bucket name must contain environment: $BUCKET_NAME"
            exit 1
          fi

      - name: Validate encryption enabled
        run: |
          ENCRYPTION=$(terraform output -raw encryption_enabled)
          if [ "$ENCRYPTION" != "true" ]; then
            echo "Error: S3 bucket encryption must be enabled"
            exit 1
          fi
```

**outputs.tf**
```hcl
output "bucket_name" {
  value       = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id
  description = "Name of the S3 bucket for logs"
}

output "log_group_name" {
  value       = aws_cloudwatch_log_group.app_logs.name
  description = "Name of the CloudWatch log group"
}

output "encryption_enabled" {
  value       = true
  description = "Whether SSE encryption is enabled"
}
```

## Evaluation Criteria
- [ ] Wrapper module correctly injects environment context
- [ ] S3 bucket naming includes environment suffix
- [ ] Server-side encryption is enabled (AES256)
- [ ] CloudWatch log group created with appropriate retention
- [ ] Backend configured for per-environment state isolation
- [ ] CI gate validates bucket naming convention
- [ ] CI gate validates encryption is enabled

## Why This Is Asked
This checks real-world ability to design multi-cloud patterns with clean module boundaries and governance.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform modules, provider aliases, multi-cloud strategy
- Environment backends and per-environment isolation
- CI gates, version constraints, governance for cloud support

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  alias   = "aws"
  version = "~> 4.0"
  region  = var.aws_region
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "google"
  version = "~> 4.0"
  project = var.gcp_project
}
provider "azurerm" {
  alias    = "azure"
  version  = "~> 3.0"
  features {}
}
module "monitors" {
  source       = "./modules/monitors"
  cloud_config = {
    aws   = { region = var.aws_region, enabled = true }
    gcp   = { region = var.gcp_region, enabled = true }
    azure = { region = var.azure_region, enabled = true }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-cloud drift in this pattern?
- How would you extend to add a new cloud while keeping tight approvals?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design multi-cloud infrastructure with consistent module boundaries, robust state management, and policy-driven gates, reflecting real-world enterprise needs.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliases and multiple backends
- Shared module design for multi-cloud resources
- Per-environment state isolation and plan gating
- Drift Detection and policy enforcement (OPA/Sentinel)
- CI/CD integration for plan-only gates

## Code Example

```hcl
# Minimal sketch illustrating two providers with aliases and a multi-env config
provider "aws" {
  region = var.aws_region
  alias  = "aws_prod"
}
provider "google" {
  project = var.gcp_project
  region  = var.gcp_region
  alias   = "gcp_prod"
}

module "messaging" {
  source = "./modules/messaging"
  config = {
    prod = {
      aws  = { region = "us-east-1" }
      gcp  = { project = "prod-project" }
    }
    staging = {
      aws  = { region = "us-west-2" }
      gcp  = { project = "staging-project" }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle secrets rotation across clouds in Terraform?
- What are the trade-offs of using Sentinel vs OPA for this gating scenario?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests experience with governance, cross-repo data sharing, and policy-as-code in real fintech environments.

## Key Concepts
- cost-governance patterns
- terraform_remote_state data source
- policy as code (OPA)
- CI gating of plans

## Code Example
```javascript
# Minimal budget fetch (pseudo-terraform)
data "terraform_remote_state" "budget" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "gov-budgets";
    key    = "costs/${var.env}.tfstate";
    region = "us-east-1";
  }
}
locals {
  monthly_budget = data.terraform_remote_state.budget.outputs["monthly_budget"]
}
```
```javascript
# Minimal OPA policy sketch
package terraform.costs
deny[msg] {
  planned_cost := input.planned_cost
  budget := input.monthly_budget
  planned_cost > budget
  msg = sprintf("Budget exceeded: %v > %v", planned_cost, budget)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to handle multi-region budgets?
- How would you add tests to verify gating behavior in CI?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests how a junior engineer models read-only environments, data sourcing, and per-env state management without touching prod resources. It mirrors real-world patterns at scale at top tech companies.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform data sources for read-only references
- Per-env backends to segregate state
- Avoiding resource creation in isolated environments

## Code Example
```javascript
data "aws_vpc" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Environment"
    values = ["inventory"]
  }
}
data "aws_subnet" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.inventory.id]
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "inventory/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
output "inventory_vpc_id" { value = data.aws_vpc.inventory.id }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify that inventory plans never propose changes to prod data? 
- How would you automate inclusion of inventory in CI without accidentally applying prod changes?

## Concept Overview

Blue-green deployment maintains two identical production environments, enabling zero-downtime deployments by routing traffic between them while Terraform manages infrastructure state and consistency.

## Implementation Details

- **Workspaces**: Create dedicated `blue` and `green` Terraform workspaces with identical infrastructure configurations
- **State Management**: Configure remote backend with state locking to prevent concurrent modifications and ensure state consistency
- **Atlantis Integration**: Set up PR-based workflows that deploy to the inactive workspace first, with automated validation and approval gates
- **Traffic Routing**: Implement load balancer target groups to seamlessly switch traffic after passing comprehensive health checks

## Code Example

```hcl
# workspace configuration
terraform {
  backend "remote" {
    organization = "your-org"
    workspaces {
      blue = "blue-prod"
      green = "green-prod"
    }
  }
}
```
